#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/id #uuid "4b092f86-cf53-51b4-9f79-b43d58c8b2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5563" :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthonormal:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/7e263f3e5b4f2491f2f5c469a3a0f861bf2985e67fec67d3016f4bb7b53f4493.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given set as $\\{\\mathbf{w}_1,\\mathbf{w}_2\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe set $W$ is orthonormal if the following conditions hold:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/1a2b4f5d6fb8842115feb6e6f12ac70399768c94006966838c3817eabd85e7fb.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our set:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=16+16=32\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are orthogonal, and $\\mathbf w_1$ is a unit vector, but $\\mathbf w_2$ is *not* a unit vector.\n\nTherefore, ${W}$ is *not* an orthonormal set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "af1ca39e-5cb7-5a93-b88f-0ff86a563bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5564" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/b62da31df7bea27c23f8dfcf1615ae9514ed48727e8503a389abc9a55ce1d108.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/95c6967179c825684229dcdd991bc0ba13d66d2f7fa44d3a4f62b9c54092e229.png) such that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/410f07f748fa4e40decedadacd97ad7df0b5f9201c53db8f4934fd3096aea470.png)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). This means $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_3=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/0477864853171cf0afdcc87e2debb27c24efa8b6166cc396d8f7cd0a126f7ace.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/a6e3e898672afd315ae56af8a6e098565947b3cf8c7c2e86792e27e8e8775cdb.png)\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/87b1cb524aee7db1f7e6f2a34c81874d2f9dae66d94a4e7b8b5c63b3713d9da7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "7ef88417-f089-51ea-9fac-dccef5825016" :question/math-academy-id "e-7114" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/969339570bc776ebf69a7f6e9cbff0377fa35842a913e68680d8bd2ed0a1decb.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$ and\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/e7034bdd15c1adc9aca80284bb2ab9b5a99ce5f33fa65f7b867c542158727b9d.png)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). This means $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/e690911497bbee717323b680e92c57a7ab9d6d9d78d6ed3440d2657cd5883c3d.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we obtain the vector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/53cd895629e3c850067d8e443c5f7ce411d1a9358a6123bc72465fdce673f14c.png) \n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}.$ Notice that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2+1^2} = \\sqrt{3}.$ So, we normalize $\\mathbf x$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/86d8735a9101ad90efe6c83e21ba6849f488baf3202ff549e9aea92764019c0e.png)\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/24cd2c6c9a445179df93ecf534fbab1db3906a6db78473505ea0fd6c7de2e466.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/id #uuid "8a41b0e9-c029-5175-80c9-5d9c6c1dd5f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7257" :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthogonal:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/8f316d4e8c33c1d8d087f2fb42e283169919a4cbc9c14a67792df70a447a41b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "If we denote the vectors in our set ${X}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\mathbf{x}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${X}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0=2\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo ${X}$ is *not* an orthogonal set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9406119e-4d2c-575e-9aad-b75e4f52a5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352884} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4327fc-8a74-5938-b491-e81494ed6fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352885} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef3c437-7fd1-5007-b849-89afa1b00f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U,$ $V$ and $W$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352886} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2b4924-7608-53a2-a2ed-b08fb8d34cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352887} {:answer/id #uuid "52b11d19-9fea-518c-9cc9-6c98fc1c6506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9406119e-4d2c-575e-9aad-b75e4f52a5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352884} :answer-field/id #uuid "378bf095-ea7e-53a1-a97a-9ecf89cc0c00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adea282e-6aaa-5784-aa8d-026ea45a32cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140480" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=4-4+4=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=10-9-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0+3-3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=0-3+3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e11a3926-0775-556c-9ea7-4ded3db05d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352891} {:answer/id #uuid "c4cd87bf-58e0-576f-b264-6154df643e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352892} {:answer/id #uuid "86c3fa6e-6198-5f61-a461-3b7c7ca7f7b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352893} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3ed448-9b1b-5506-8777-3b4307e1c933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352894} {:answer/id #uuid "6f22ef99-bcf4-5ac8-9b1b-b36611b1801f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c3fa6e-6198-5f61-a461-3b7c7ca7f7b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352893} :answer-field/id #uuid "84064a4e-ebc9-5603-b1b0-c9c6f1062731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80594410-bd8a-57ed-8b86-b3651312971c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140484" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6}{13}-\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=4+9=13\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{4}{29}-\\frac{25}{29}=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4247c1b-b6f4-5170-a329-93489cdcc033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352898} {:answer/id #uuid "82fccd32-568c-5947-b890-6cc6e4f75918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352899} {:answer/id #uuid "ca65aafc-e741-562a-af9f-cffb83812c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352900} {:answer/id #uuid "cee2d305-15bf-5f7c-962f-68da3579b03d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352901} {:answer/id #uuid "48c403e5-5750-5f8c-b0f8-ed9677d881fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48c403e5-5750-5f8c-b0f8-ed9677d881fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352902} :answer-field/id #uuid "416b5cc6-80b2-5aff-a0fa-8a3d36991ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96c998e0-f627-54a3-a7d5-35c85c329e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-140485" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3(\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{1}=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb886aa7-99d2-556e-b80b-c2e7f12c3f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352905} {:answer/id #uuid "918e0271-4f3b-55fa-89be-02e7a9a79184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352906} {:answer/id #uuid "2b07817c-b525-52e4-981a-0355986a107c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352907} {:answer/id #uuid "8f50d622-038f-590b-aaa8-6841e0353f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352908} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6f66f0-0201-5da6-9e95-55bcb67ca9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f50d622-038f-590b-aaa8-6841e0353f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352908} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebea7b1a-601a-5ebf-9ee6-4a51cddaaacd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c1907b8-bbf2-5329-b7a6-f92c1f1333cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140488" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{10}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc372ce8-2c01-537a-bf92-066695baeb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352912} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5b9c7c-067a-5b40-9498-c578f26a27a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352913} {:answer/id #uuid "61587816-0e0f-5278-bff4-5d2616510f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352914} {:answer/id #uuid "00763332-aeb9-50fc-8e1f-7deefc8c45f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352915} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fd33c8-622b-5988-8ccc-762fc9399773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61587816-0e0f-5278-bff4-5d2616510f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352914} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c76283-254c-5d81-91f9-70165916e974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7200cf5f-505a-51d3-8296-b8fb9e058960" :question/math-academy-id "q-39396" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=6-16+10=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=-8+10=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=15-12-1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0af6271-c27b-541e-9dde-5f993f186c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131283} {:answer/id #uuid "f856ced4-de4a-541f-80b6-270c8d208a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131284} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6b0ea1-b95e-5f27-b3e0-640034849bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131285} {:answer/id #uuid "03476a76-f102-5586-b40b-89089d81f861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131286} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd5c09c-51b3-5bf0-a385-8194349a2053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bd5c09c-51b3-5bf0-a385-8194349a2053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131287} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc768b9f-1293-5902-8743-932c9682fc48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89970297-1b2c-5ba8-b18f-ccd95df5c317" :question/math-academy-id "q-39398" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{1}{10}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=4+9+1=14\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{12}{25}-\\frac{12}{25}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "871728bf-d0dd-594a-a4df-f135c8f90079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131313} {:answer/id #uuid "022d8317-b5f2-5f62-8c1c-2fe367d530cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131314} {:answer/id #uuid "11c23da6-2cf1-51c7-9323-73d7d90d00c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131315} {:answer/id #uuid "161b90b4-7a78-5f7f-bd9b-f95acd23697f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131316} {:answer/id #uuid "31df63b9-5c40-5fc0-a414-f3c9a08e94d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "161b90b4-7a78-5f7f-bd9b-f95acd23697f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131316} :answer-field/id #uuid "011a3caf-290a-5d12-b0f8-9977c99323d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12204de2-0478-5c7f-9522-6daf958e7975" :question/math-academy-id "q-39480" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{3}=-2{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{4})+(-2{x}_{4})+{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{4} \\\\ -{x}_{4} \\\\ -2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3349e75-09c2-5884-a4ce-052856d9ff0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131673} {:answer/id #uuid "dea320f5-4c61-547b-af99-b900efc33bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131674} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cab8ee-13da-5fc7-9a96-16992918ff9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131675} {:answer/id #uuid "1bef498b-3695-5179-b964-085768e03095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131676} {:answer/id #uuid "6235696f-c34d-5ac7-80c1-d4d4056833e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5cab8ee-13da-5fc7-9a96-16992918ff9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131675} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ce97c3-2de2-5a97-b4d1-c06708bd1f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "455820f5-9352-535f-a3b2-e3fbc55031b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40297" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above, which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8229da1f-6f53-5a98-a273-dfffaa426652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352919} {:answer/id #uuid "a66b6833-f86e-5afd-b704-c29951d79d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352920} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbb3e01-53ba-5522-ae46-6ba6420bffe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352921} {:answer/id #uuid "d4276c84-2827-57db-aac7-c6df56241fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352922} {:answer/id #uuid "a883cac9-038e-5b26-a479-8558e96e3f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a883cac9-038e-5b26-a479-8558e96e3f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352923} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d89e571-4266-582d-8fe4-48a16c2e3884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7002ea68-32ba-5517-83f4-9ef3c4549a92" :question/math-academy-id "q-40299" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4})-{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=5$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adfa01f5-5f50-5732-a9db-ac0621c041cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133071} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d6d15e-984d-5cc1-8a0a-28097dea73fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133072} {:answer/id #uuid "e72e8102-ebfc-5bbd-a48a-99e7c0980a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133073} {:answer/id #uuid "2c75ffbf-3ef4-5005-92fe-66944038f963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133074} {:answer/id #uuid "53621beb-a184-5c08-8412-4681af661920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c75ffbf-3ef4-5005-92fe-66944038f963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133074} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36fb41c-582a-5bd7-af72-45377347516d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d56008d-b77e-5ef1-b9fa-ea3db550aefd" :question/math-academy-id "q-81011" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\sqrt{13}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}|=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b29d5d-4a22-5353-be43-060388a35327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352926} {:answer/id #uuid "6861ec02-a13f-554b-b9fb-32dc2bce9f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352927} {:answer/id #uuid "40a06e60-3da0-5fee-a32a-2f9c8c68c98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352928} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb755b4-1961-5662-889a-786a379f3c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352929} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d397c7-b17c-5495-bb75-530b8fa38635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6861ec02-a13f-554b-b9fb-32dc2bce9f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352927} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aee4e82-3e74-5c3d-8489-7a7e325b4b51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d80cbd9a-e77a-5108-ac36-72cd3ed5f0f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81109" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot {u}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1 \\\\ {w}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ec0d37-6054-5896-949d-6ea84abf3e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352933} {:answer/id #uuid "5eca89dc-ea14-5e21-91f0-e71ae4acfba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352934} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8a6f02-a19d-5421-8f55-754e40af620b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352935} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac70841-1b79-5a4d-911f-627e1e3e8ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352936} {:answer/id #uuid "e305a5e3-aba4-5e74-b261-492f3a0ecff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84ec0d37-6054-5896-949d-6ea84abf3e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352933} :answer-field/id #uuid "3db9a2ce-3526-5707-a596-4c81688f0223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fca8409-6035-5535-87d2-9ec23d0bda51" :question/math-academy-id "q-81114" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e112f56d-f0b5-559f-8257-c193201d8019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{35}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352940} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dab33a-7cbd-5790-aa68-54902b5db5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352941} {:answer/id #uuid "75352235-fe8d-56c5-9d46-b59c2683f340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352942} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad2a5d3-d161-5ec8-8ae6-08ca6e04e7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352943} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3ab3b5-451d-53f5-a0f2-2779bcf2cca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e112f56d-f0b5-559f-8257-c193201d8019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{35}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352940} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c42b2df-8d3c-5263-82f9-eed267c80263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd7e2b49-e015-5da5-97ce-63ab84487079" :question/math-academy-id "q-81117" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{10}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{14}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+3(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{35}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}|=\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{35}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6ebabd9-2247-5907-afd6-10e1f2ee0f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352947} {:answer/id #uuid "06bb32a6-20a4-58fd-9811-86956aa30ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352948} {:answer/id #uuid "2db4a1c7-3132-5e39-9b02-5fa0ac42988c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352949} {:answer/id #uuid "cef9ee24-9dc0-5b26-9b46-161a9c3032f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352950} {:answer/id #uuid "34597237-688d-528c-95ba-10b85c18591a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34597237-688d-528c-95ba-10b85c18591a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352951} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfae2e2e-f88c-54c1-aa06-6cba2fb75799" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7846bd8-7435-58cb-bdf7-37d4247e0864" :question/math-academy-id "q-82396" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-10-3+0=-13\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-18-2=-20\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2-1-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0-1+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+1-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e027f2e-e9a0-5b8f-a460-f3fe05113e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352954} {:answer/id #uuid "20f38fcf-9181-515f-abae-cf3d74922a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352955} {:answer/id #uuid "b1142ecb-cea7-5d54-be7b-a6b80307ec31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352956} {:answer/id #uuid "53c0b04a-8658-5a50-a3da-abf81c739694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352957} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1df854-3999-56fa-98f9-ffcea6dccd34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1df854-3999-56fa-98f9-ffcea6dccd34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352958} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a1f73f-be8b-5c97-9656-af3031f13b76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "791094d3-0869-53a1-bd62-582e012d4cec" :question/math-academy-id "q-82397" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-1+3-1-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=1+0-2+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1+0+2-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2-1-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=-15+24=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67dab52e-dd02-5178-b7b0-6dca9d782e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352961} {:answer/id #uuid "e58b3553-282a-5976-bd66-c5fd5f98727f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352962} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab7b132-3de3-58c4-98e4-1d851966b4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352963} {:answer/id #uuid "65ee5285-f4e8-5344-815f-74b6868627e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352964} {:answer/id #uuid "bce6a255-d956-5b7b-a1a4-6e2eb2f4dd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce6a255-d956-5b7b-a1a4-6e2eb2f4dd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352965} :answer-field/id #uuid "86674a77-0257-5585-b78e-63b7d03fd95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34116245-ce13-58c7-b0ef-7a4168b6e425" :question/math-academy-id "q-82401" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=1+1=2\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=1+0=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+1=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9d50ab-0418-578c-a616-2a9c87b4b5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352968} {:answer/id #uuid "83f47a32-f1cb-57f1-b36a-a6fe84230d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352969} {:answer/id #uuid "1e018e13-e8d9-5fdf-96af-f0ee2aa3be01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352970} {:answer/id #uuid "90d299d0-091f-562a-b944-63c65d0705a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352971} {:answer/id #uuid "1463e04b-8a4c-50d1-88de-13e82c028330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f9d50ab-0418-578c-a616-2a9c87b4b5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352968} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcdf2b2e-4f90-5cfb-8d15-39df6c5db160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a49bdd8-1b47-5f19-a5c1-f6bf1e352872" :question/math-academy-id "q-83719" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1+0+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=12+0+1=13\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]=-40+40=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208c607d-7f92-5871-a206-4fb48a7bd71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352975} {:answer/id #uuid "ae649ed9-0fe4-5f8f-ac89-4bbc4eef7330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352976} {:answer/id #uuid "0f3f89d6-888b-592f-baee-731c4c5c8490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352977} {:answer/id #uuid "b9584e86-bc05-5ff7-85e3-710fbfcff284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352978} {:answer/id #uuid "9cced40f-8e2a-56e2-b80d-bbd8ee748e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "208c607d-7f92-5871-a206-4fb48a7bd71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352975} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3331e1-e576-59d5-9720-1defdcf53068" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26a7cf30-8a66-5018-a53e-6ace9edd956a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83745" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=0+1+8=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fdabf14-a362-5508-af3f-adb65ea0ceae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352982} {:answer/id #uuid "edd2c9ce-cbcd-5d2a-98aa-bccb9c2dc6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352983} {:answer/id #uuid "05fb02d2-2c37-5f1f-82b6-83b914aa3df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352984} {:answer/id #uuid "f54ec74f-c3c9-521f-b6f2-660876e2ea3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352985} {:answer/id #uuid "e117aadf-3cbf-5cb0-a1ae-14f01bd5ba75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edd2c9ce-cbcd-5d2a-98aa-bccb9c2dc6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352983} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab297600-23e7-543f-a2eb-aff4fd05fe86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "713d67ee-e475-52f9-bd91-775ea12c967d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83747" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(-3{x}_{3})+4{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{-3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b93219c-eb15-54af-b13e-09f4cf658f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352989} {:answer/id #uuid "b64428af-a427-5c84-8fb9-3e5fa1cbfb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352990} {:answer/id #uuid "5f48638b-b8ea-5bf5-9c25-817e4a616ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352991} {:answer/id #uuid "3118939f-07c0-5ccf-9d12-b1e154fbf294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352992} {:answer/id #uuid "de3a844e-3797-5f24-8e94-1791bf1e7ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b64428af-a427-5c84-8fb9-3e5fa1cbfb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352990} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2d55033-2007-5436-a931-a60a79f0c1f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ccf01290-02e2-5c4e-9863-5d516274aa67" :question/math-academy-id "q-83763" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{5}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}|+|-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}|=\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{30}}.$"}]}
